From: lagz0ne <15101444+lagz0ne@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Tue, 17 Nov 2026 04:35:50 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 50/56] feat(mcp): agents see where the people on a canvas are
 looking

An agent connected over MCP could read any frame it named, but not the
one a person meant by "this frame" or "the one I'm on". The room already
knows: each person's selected frame (the editing message), their camera
(viewport, which follow uses) and their pointer. The MCP server never
read any of it, so the agent had to guess or ask for a link.

get_focus answers it. For each person on the canvas now: the selected
frame, the page and world rect on their screen, the frames in view
ranked by how much of the screen each fills (up to 12, with the share of
each on screen), the frame under the pointer, and focus, the frame to
take as theirs (the selection, else what fills the screen most). you:
true marks the account the agent acts for. Without canvas_id it lists
the readable canvases that have people on them. get_canvas carries the
same list as people, and the agent instructions say when to call it.

About 100-300 tokens of text per person, against 3-13k for a frame's
HTML or ~1.7k for its screenshot: the agent can find the frame first,
then look at only that one.

Prior art: Figma's Dev Mode MCP server works on the current selection in
the desktop app when no node id is given.

People only: silent sessions (view-as, signed-out guests) and agents are
left out, and access is the canvas's view role as for get_canvas. A
viewer's selection is not tracked by the room (editing is editor-only),
so for them focus comes from the camera. The room registers a reader at
startup (server/roomFocus.ts), so mcp.ts does not import the room.

Tests: mcpFocus.test.ts (a real socket's camera, pointer and selection
read back over MCP; another account sees nothing), the parity list.
